#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50b9aa5d-1540-5570-a58c-3ea91f88966d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318640} {:answer/id #uuid "0d00dc17-b7a2-5304-b27d-74453684d381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318641} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a0250e-5663-59fd-a9bb-8322e581b403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318642} {:answer/id #uuid "3772762f-807c-5a76-8b08-94d7227dc126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318643} {:answer/id #uuid "a02b09dc-c0ce-555c-8de1-44295f60cf3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50b9aa5d-1540-5570-a58c-3ea91f88966d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318640} :answer-field/id #uuid "a067b33e-b3c3-54ef-bb49-636c14cb4dd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6df953a-e0ea-50a9-90ed-096dcc70da47" :question/math-academy-id "q-116612" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4} & ={x}^{2}(4-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2-x)(2+x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(2-x)(2+x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=2.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(2,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2+x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ef8e42b-dd3d-5f65-8dd8-afeaec69dfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318647} {:answer/id #uuid "960023ce-6c22-5b9b-9783-27c17787f677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "960023ce-6c22-5b9b-9783-27c17787f677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318648} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b953e31-a404-575e-a663-8686105a1509" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f88d3fc-fc28-50fb-8e4e-e824df01bbba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f88d3fc-fc28-50fb-8e4e-e824df01bbba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318650} :answer-field/id #uuid "637fc4be-db86-51fe-894d-dac22365a833" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdc1bcec-90d2-5286-8dfa-ef1e66c75d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-281744" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-2{x}^{3}+16\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{3}+16 & =-2({x}^{3}-8) \\\\  & =-2(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$ The solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=2.$  This root divides the number line into $2$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,2)\\,,\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+2x+4$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-\\infty ,2).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the root is also a solution, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality when the Right-Hand Side is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e225b53e-81af-58bb-8ca7-e82f811439a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e225b53e-81af-58bb-8ca7-e82f811439a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318653} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dcdfcdd-76ab-51ab-bad8-ab44ab0881ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be97b26-3083-5466-ba2e-8a4ce54f69e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0be97b26-3083-5466-ba2e-8a4ce54f69e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318655} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a4d6cb-a5fb-5ede-8ec8-8e134a471449" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318654} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e18a944-f7a5-54c4-90ee-dfe2fae70b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e18a944-f7a5-54c4-90ee-dfe2fae70b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318657} :answer-field/id #uuid "d909e8be-2f13-5b87-b7c4-f1664b7912ba" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4135142b-80bb-55a2-aad2-d3b8f234d474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4135142b-80bb-55a2-aad2-d3b8f234d474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318659} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b4dc1d-d13f-5c36-8271-b48b52683a7e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2bc5c3a-2d4c-54b4-b715-106b2d4bfd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2bc5c3a-2d4c-54b4-b715-106b2d4bfd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318661} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c4d23e2-afdf-5347-9919-87e1f991e0aa" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2df07d8c-b2fb-594c-8103-3989dd07f7cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-282514" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}>4{x}^{5}+8{x}^{4}.$\n\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃\\,($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)⋃\\,($\n{{field-4}}$,$\n{{field-5}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} & >4{x}^{5}+8{x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-4{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4} & >0 \\\\ -4{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-4{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+x+2) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2}(x+2)+(x+2)) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(-4{x}^{2}+1) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(1-4{x}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(1-2x)(1+2x) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+2)(1-2x)(1+2x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ These roots divide the number line into $5$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $1-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $1+2x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)⋃(-\\frac{1}{2},0)⋃(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c8f3d7-32ab-5873-967b-ed7336c643f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318664} {:answer/id #uuid "37e188d6-2a16-548a-b5b2-3b831fcc3519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318665} {:answer/id #uuid "b48e944b-6448-55e5-9d13-e3f45f4e7109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318666} {:answer/id #uuid "dc52be97-b6a0-5cb6-8034-6e05cbba1eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,-1)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318667} {:answer/id #uuid "1eebc8d6-e544-53c8-a65b-f9ccb9e7062b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,0)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b48e944b-6448-55e5-9d13-e3f45f4e7109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318666} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bbff3e8-4997-5e0a-8107-4afba22dd723" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09e851f1-b681-584d-81f8-586cfcdbdf9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40225" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}-16x<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-16x & =x({x}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =x(x+4)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x(x+4)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-4,$ $x=0$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(0,4).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (0,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c0ba8ce-7726-5ef9-8b39-c81ab4dd4433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-9]\\cup [9,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318671} {:answer/id #uuid "b93ba36b-2ece-5c42-b7ed-47ae391c65f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-9,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318672} {:answer/id #uuid "fefb577e-776c-5b73-a52b-f02e11a2145f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318673} {:answer/id #uuid "e9285010-571b-5d4e-8fd8-dd5190b5f5aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [3,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318674} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c4b192-65dd-5919-af56-55f1561c2972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4c4b192-65dd-5919-af56-55f1561c2972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318675} :answer-field/id #uuid "e97c3e16-8ca6-54e2-9960-2fdbd706b35c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16d2ac84-9ec4-527e-a345-d5f61486154a" :question/math-academy-id "q-40368" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $({x}^{2}+3)(x+3)(x-3)\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=({x}^{2}+3)(x+3)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-3$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+3$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$"}]}
